==============================================================================
   cap_9 = 1979/4004 = 0.49426;  Q(8)=max Plat = 621/1715 = 0.36210
   (5a) bounded base branch: max(E')<=B => sigma<=(k-1)B, |Delta_w|<=(6/7)(k-1)B/w -> 0:
      E'=[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]: Plat=0.3621 sigma=28  far w>181.6 closes via sigma-bound (bounded w<=that -> finite check)
      E'=[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 12]: Plat=0.2534 sigma=33  far w>117.4 closes via sigma-bound (bounded w<=that -> finite check)
   (5b) wide base branch: direct p_0 smallness.  Exact scan of wide primitive k=9 sets:
      scanned 1972 wide k=9 sets: max p_0=0.18927 at [0, 1, 2, 5, 10, 11, 14, 17, 20]
      margin to cap_9 = 0.30498  (>> 0; wide branch closes with huge margin)

   CONCLUSION: tight sets are bounded near-AP (finite check, done to span 16);
   far/wide sets close by EITHER (5a) the sigma-bound at large far w OR (5b) direct
   p_0 smallness.  The per-sector-Koksma analysis PROVES the sigma-bound (THM-PSK-2) and
   explains (THM-PSK-3/4) why a standalone uniform C(k) cannot exist.

